\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Dura (ISO 639-3 \texttt{drq}; Glottocode dura1244) is a Sino-Tibetan language formerly spoken in Lamjung District, Nepal, called Dur\=a Bh\~u by the community. It is dormant or extinct; speakers have shifted to Nepali. Census counts of ethnic Dura cited on Wikipedia vary (for example 2,160 in 2011 and other figures), and these are not speaker counts.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No school materials were found. Dictionaries by Ke\'s Bah\=adur Dur\=a (1994, 2003) are mentioned in a review, but no access link was found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia on Dura: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dura_language}
\item Wikipedia on Greater Magaric languages (Schorer's proposed grouping): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_Magaric_languages}
\item Wikipedia on Tandrange, a closely related language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tandrange_language}
\item Review in \emph{Himalayan Linguistics} (mentions the Dur\=a dictionaries): \url{https://journalpub.escholarship.org/himalayanlinguistics/article/35165/galley/26182/download}
\item Schorer, The phylogenetic position of the Dura language of Nepal (BORIS, Bern): \url{https://boris.unibe.ch/73504/}
\item Library record for Schorer's grammar and phylogeny book (Deakin): \url{https://library2.deakin.edu.au/record=b4172959~S1}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Numerals data page for Dura, MPI-EVA: \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Dura.htm}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
With no first-language speakers, any curriculum would be revitalisation or heritage-oriented, built on Schorer's documentation and the dictionaries. No existing school programme was found.

\end{document}
